  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Near normal temperatures and isolated to widely \nscattered afternoon/evening showers and storms are expected to \ncontinue through the weekend. \n\nAfternoon wx analysis shows a weakening upper low over KY/TN/GA, \nwith southerly flow aloft over the Mid-Atlantic. Yesterday's \nshortwave/stationary front is now well to our north. While skies are \npartly to mostly cloudy with cumulus, there are only a few showers \non radar (mainly in SE VA/NE NC) thanks to height rises from the \nbuilding ridge offshore and no boundaries/well defined forcing \nmechanism nearby. Convection may increase in coverage a little bit \nduring the next couple of hours across the southeast, though the \nvast majority of the area should stay dry. While widespread flooding \nis not expected, even a quick half inch or inch of precip may be \nenough to cause localized issues in areas that saw so much rainfall \nyesterday.  The drying trend continues on Thursday with even more \nheight rises as the ridge offshore builds to the west. There is only \na slight chance for an isolated shower or storm during the \naftn/evening (mainly in the Piedmont). With lower storm chances, \ntemps rebound to the upper 80s-lower 90s on Thursday with upper 90s-\nlow 100s heat indices. Similar conditions are expected Friday into \nthe weekend with some slight height falls over the local area as the \nupper ridge offshore retreats a bit to the SE and a series of weak \nshortwaves pass by to our N/NW. This will lead to slightly greater \nchances for diurnally driven showers or storms on Friday and \nSaturday across western and northern portions of the area but PoPs \ngenerally remain aob 30%. A few storms are possible on Sunday, with \nthe highest precip chances (still only 20-30%) shifting to SE VA/NE \nNC. Seasonable heat and humidity will continue through the weekend, \nwith heat indices in the upper 90s-low 100s each day.",
